{
  "name": "Chromatic Aberration",
  "notes": "The lens fault, built the way the fault is: ONE shape drawn three times, each copy displaced, each carrying one channel. Three `line` emitters share a centre driven by the same LFO and differ only in the `translate` under two of them, so the bar moves as one object and its edges separate. `screen` rather than `add` -- three saturated channels at full weight under add spend their time clipped, and the whole point is the fringes.\n\nNot R/G/B. A split complement -- cerise at 315 degrees, cobalt at 220, and acid lime at 78 as the odd one out -- so the fringes are a colour relationship rather than a test card, and the lime lands on one edge only.\n\nTwo chains. `M2` drives `M1.rate`, so the split does not sweep at a constant speed: the separation races and stalls on a noise clock, which is what an optical fault looks like as a lens moves. And `M7` is a `macro` signal, not a rack knob -- its `knob` is a plain 0..1 matrix source, so ONE rack control fans out to three rates at three different depths and the detuning between them survives the sweep. A rack knob with four targets would have had to set them equal. `M6` does the same for hue, with OPPOSITE signs on the three layers, so turning Colour walks the three channels through analogous, split and complementary rather than rotating all three together.",
